{
  "id": "ur_practical",
  "title": "Urdu — practical English romanization (Pakistani style)",
  "language": "ur",
  "script": "Arab",
  "kind": "practical",
  "status": "informal",
  "ascii": true,
  "authority": "Pakistani CNIC/passport and press practice (Hunterian/BGN-PCGN without diacritics); Ministry of Religious Affairs notification (1979) on the spelling Muhammad; not a formal standard",
  "description": "Plain-ASCII spellings as written on Pakistani documents: Muhammad, Hussain, Rehman, Majeed, Mehmood, Syed (lexicon); ee/oo for medial ī/ū, e/o for majhūl vowels, ai/au, w for و, ق = q, final silent h = a (Fatima, Ayesha), Arabic article as a separate capitalised word (Zia Ur Rehman, Chiragh Ud Din), Abdul + name, -ullah joined (Amanullah). Known names come from the lexicon (latin['ur']).",
  "sources": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pakistani_name",
    "https://gsp.gov.pk/wp-content/uploads/2025/05/DD-G.pdf", "https://jang.com.pk/news/490894",
    "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5ab4e64d40f0b67d64e21540/ROMANIZATION_OF_URDU.pdf",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Urdu"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Lexicon spellings win; the rules only handle vocalised input and names the lexicon does not know.",
    "a before h/ḥ closing a syllable is written e (Rehman, Mehmood, Shehzad, Zehra), except at the start of a word (Ahmad) — the usual Pakistani spelling, attested in official candidate lists (Ur Rehman).",
    "ī and ū are ee/oo inside a word (Majeed, Adeel, Maqbool) and i/u at the end (Ali, Abu); 'Din' keeps i in Ud Din (as on CNIC records).",
    "s between short vowels is doubled (Hussain, Hassan), except after a word-initial vowel (Asad).",
    "Consonantal و is written w (Waqas, Rizwan); the v spelling (Javed, Pervez) is lexical."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤئابپتٹثجچحخدڈذرڑزژسشصضطظعغفقکگلمنںوہھیےۃ",
